      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Planning Checklist for 12GB GPUs: Model Weights, KV Cache, and Runtime",
      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical checklist for VRAM planning on a 12 GB GPU for running local LLMs. The scenario is purely hypothetical and has not been tested with actual models or runtimes. The goal is to provide a structured approach to estimating VRAM needs.\n\nWe'll separate allocations into three key areas:\n\n1.  **Model Weights:** This is the primary consumer, varying significantly by model size (e.g., 7B, 13B) and quantization level (e.g., FP16, 4-bit). For a 12 GB card, larger models will likely require aggressive quantization.\n2.  **KV Cache:** This scales with context length and batch size. Longer contexts and larger batches demand more VRAM. Planning for a reasonable context window is crucial.\n3.  **Other Runtime Allocations:** This includes the operating system, the LLM inference framework itself (e.g., llama.cpp, vLLM), and any intermediate activations or buffers. These are generally smaller but can add up.\n\nThis framework aims to help users anticipate potential VRAM bottlenecks before attempting to load a model. Remember, no specific model or runtime was tested; this is a conceptual guide.",
