      "title": "Structuring VRAM Usage: A Hypothetical Guide for 12GB Local LLM Setups",
      "body": "This guide presents a hypothetical framework for VRAM management on a 12 GB GPU when running local LLMs. It's crucial to understand that this is a conceptual exercise; no specific models or runtimes were evaluated, and these are not measured results. The objective is to offer a structured way to think about VRAM allocation.\n\nWe propose categorizing VRAM usage into three distinct components:\n\n*   **Model Weights:** The size of the model's parameters is the largest factor, heavily influenced by the parameter count (e.g., 7B, 13B) and the precision used (e.g., FP16, INT8, 4-bit quantization). A 12 GB card will necessitate careful consideration of quantization for larger models.\n*   **KV Cache:** This dynamic allocation grows with the sequence length of the input and the batch size. Longer conversations or processing multiple requests concurrently will increase KV cache requirements.\n*   **Runtime Overhead:** This encompasses the VRAM consumed by the operating system, the inference engine (like Transformers, llama.cpp), and any temporary buffers or activations generated during processing.\n\nBy considering these categories hypothetically, users can better anticipate potential VRAM constraints before deploying models. This is a planning aid, not a performance benchmark.",
